buildabook: a book builder driven by a plain-text outline
===========================================================

buildabook turns a plain-text outline into a typeset book. One outline file
drives the chapter numbering, the table of contents, and every cross-reference,
so a book is a build artifact of a single source of order.

The program reads `outline.txt` (the one source of sequence and numbering),
resolves `{{token}}` cross-references inside the chapter source files, and
emits a Typst `book.typ` plus a `number_map.csv` audit. It grew out of three
book projects that had each accumulated a near-identical copy of the same
build script; this is that script, factored out and written in Ada.

The input language
------------------

Each non-comment line of `outline.txt` is either an entry or a directive.

    c:00001  The Hierarchy of Being     a content entry (prefix, UUID, title)
    c:label:Warrant|Wt                  a directive (prefix, name, value)
    k:00003.1  Stack 2.1: Mathematics   a sub-entry under the nearest chapter

Prefixes are `c` (chapter), `p` (part), `a` (appendix), `d` (division),
`n` (unnumbered), `s` (section), and `k` (sub-entry). A UUID is opaque
identity, never renumbered; numbering comes from row position. Directives set
state for the entries of their prefix that follow them, and that state is
snapshotted onto each entry, so a cross-reference always renders with the
regime in force at the target's position. Comments begin with `#`.

The full specification is `LANGUAGE.md`.

Building
--------

buildabook is Ada 2022, built with gprbuild, and links the hbnf runtime
(`hbnf_config.gpr`, which pulls in mustache-ada transitively). The canonical
toolchain is the ada-on-alpine overlay, packaged as the `ada-toolchain:edge`
container image.

    ./configure
    make
    make check        # the aunit suite
    make install      # installs buildabook and its man page

`configure` checks for gprbuild, the GNAT compiler, and that the hbnf and
mustache-ada projects are gprbuild-visible. `build.sh` wraps the whole build in
the container if you prefer that to a host toolchain.

Usage
-----

    buildabook render        outline -> rendered/book.typ + number_map.csv
    buildabook check         validate outline, filemap, and tokens; emit nothing
    buildabook number-map    write number_map.csv only
    buildabook spine <pdf>   rewrite the cover spine width from a page count
    buildabook build         render, then compile the PDF with Typst

The template
------------

The engine ships a default Typst template (`template/book.typ`). A book may
override it. The coupling contract an override must honour is documented in
`LANGUAGE.md`: define `book-title`, `running-title`, `fm-pages`, `body-pages`,
`chapter(path, token, part, toc)`, `pgan(id)`, `pageof(id)`, and import cmarker.

Repository hooks
----------------

The three de-claude git hooks in `githooks/` strip and block commits that credit
or mention Claude or Anthropic. Enable them in a clone with:

    make install-hooks        # or: git config core.hooksPath githooks

